WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.549
Hello, this is Torsten Steinbach, Architect
at IBM for Data and Analytics in the Cloud

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:10.140
and I'm going to talk to you today about the
SQL sandwich.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.200
This is actually about databases and object
storage.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:21.490
So, as you can see in the image, we're putting
it together as a nice and tasty sandwich.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:28.839
So, at the center of the sandwich we have
our data warehouse.

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:38.340
So, this is a very, well established mechanism
of storing and analyzing data, big data but

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:44.620
we're surrounding it now with more recent
technology and it is especially popular in

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:53.250
the cloud and object storage.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:01:02.020
We have it as the bonds basically for a sandwich
at the top and at the bottom.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:10.470
Now, what's the purpose of having all of these
components?

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:16.880
What we have actually at the top is our initial
place where we are landing and storing all

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.470
of our raw data.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:27.270
So, this might be things like log messages
from applications, IoT messages from devices

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:31.729
that are just coming in and in a pretty raw
format, we're able to just store them in the

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:39.530
object storage for very little money in a
highly elastic manner.

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:49.810
And in the data warehouse however we want
to have high quality data.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:55.189
Because the data warehouse is a much more
sophisticated but also much more expensive

00:01:55.189 --> 00:02:00.670
component to operate a host and to purchase.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:04.200
And at the bottom again, why do we have another
object storage at the bottom?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.519
Well, this is where we have our archived data.

00:02:09.519 --> 00:02:17.120
Now, all of these components basically makes
sense in an end-to-end big data analytics

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.849
use case because we have new data arriving
and being stored in the object storage, and

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:30.830
here in object storage basically we are going
to explore the data.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:40.090
So, we try to find out what's actually in
the data, and we prep the data, we prepare

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:46.379
the data, cleanse it, make it higher quality
and more curated and we also conduct to some

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:57.980
extent batch analytics directly at the data
that is stored in the object storage.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:11.790
In a data warehouse however we are basically
able to do interactive analytics that requires

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:18.610
certain SLAs for latencies of poor performance
response times and so on.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:23.299
And why are you having this archived data
again, well this is basically because you

00:03:23.299 --> 00:03:27.680
do not want to keep all of the data for years
around in a data warehouse because as I told

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.599
you this is the more expensive thing to run
with all of these components.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:36.239
So, it makes sense for the data that is not
hot anymore and is not required for your daily

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:41.189
business to archive it off again into an object
storage.

00:03:41.189 --> 00:03:46.939
So, for instance you can still run things
like compliance reports that you're required

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:56.209
to do to be compliant with certain regulations.

00:03:56.209 --> 00:04:03.019
So, these are the different types of analytics
and some things that you want to do with the

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:08.549
data at the different stages of its life cycle
basically.

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:15.260
Of course, a real sandwich should not be this
dry so you will have some sauce in there.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:19.250
So, what is the source basically that glues
these things together.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.860
It is basically an ETL mechanism.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:31.220
It allows you basically to read data from
here and transform it so that it can be read

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.410
here and same from here to here.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:42.940
So, basically data is traveling this way.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:50.600
Now finally you see a sandwich is often something
that you do not eat into pieces right, you

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.570
do not just eat the patty or just the bun.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:54.610
You eat it as a whole thing.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:05:00.530
So, we consume it as a whole thing and it's
also the same analogy that holds here that

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:05.440
while it makes sense to prepare a system that
can serve these different types of workloads

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.690
in the most efficient way and cost effective
way possible, it's of course hard if you always

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:15.670
have to think about do I have to go here,
here, or here for this query.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:20.360
It should be automatically figured out by
the system and for that reason we are putting

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.080
this all into a nice box, putting it all together.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:32.530
And we are using federation as a mechanism
on top of all of that basically virtualizes

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:40.490
the location of the data depending on its
age or state off the pipeline that it is in.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:44.340
Now finally it is called a SQL sandwich, why
is it called a SQL sandwich?

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.750
Well because SQL is the essential thing for
all of these things that you can see here.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:57.280
SQL is used for basically doing these analytics,
these are SQL queries that we are running

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:58.280
here.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:05.350
SQL is also used to do detail.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:10.710
And finally, SQL is also used in order to
federate those things together, It's a SQL

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:11.710
federation.

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:17.940
Okay, that's our SQL sandwich and I hope this
helps you to put in perspective the roles

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.560
of object storage and data warehouses and
how these two things can be put together into

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:31.690
this nice and tasty format so that you can
get the most out of the technology in combination.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:32.600
Thank you very much.

